.TH CLRUCACHE_SET_FULL 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clrucache_set_full \- store a value by key (write-through)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clrucache.h>

ccol_retval_t clrucache_set_full(clru_cache cache,
                                 const cmap_pair *key_pair,
                                 const cmap_pair *val_pair);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Pairs key_pair with val_pair in the cache.

If the caller gave a remote setter at construction, it is called
synchronously, outside the segment's lock, before the cache is
updated. If the setter fails, the function returns
ccol_unexpected_failure and nothing changes for that key: the existing
value stays, and so does the key's exact position in the eviction
order of its segment. So a set that stored nothing gets no protection
from eviction, and it cannot take from another key its place as the
next entry to evict.

Every allocation of the call, the copy of the new value included,
happens before the remote setter runs. A ccol_not_enough_memory or
ccol_container_full result means the remote setter was never called
and nothing changed for the key. Once the remote setter succeeds the
update of the cache cannot fail, so the cache never serves a value
older than the one the remote store holds.

The set takes effect when the remote setter returns. While the setter
runs, the key keeps its old value, its place in the eviction order and
its place in clrucache_size(). A concurrent insert of another key that
needs room evicts the least recently used entry, which can be the key
of the set, and the eviction callback then gets the old value. If the
setter then fails the key stays evicted. If it succeeds, the key comes
back with the new value as the most recently used entry of its
segment, which can evict another key. The outcome is always the one of
the insert followed by the set, and a segment never holds more entries
than its capacity.

Several setters of the same key are serialised, and a concurrent
getter of the same key blocks until the set completes. If the set
fails and the key is then absent from the cache, that getter goes on
as a cache miss, so it runs the remote getter when the cache has one.

When the cache is at its capacity and the key is new, the least
recently used live entry is evicted first. A set that succeeds for a
key the cache already holds makes that key the most recently used
entry of its segment.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cache     Cache handle.
key_pair  The key to store. The ptr and size fields must be set.
val_pair  The value to pair with the key. The ptr and size fields must
          be set, and size must be > 0.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success             The function stored the value, and the remote
                         setter succeeded, if the caller gave one.
ccol_unexpected_failure  The remote setter returned false. The set
                         changed nothing, but a concurrent insert can
                         have evicted the key (see above).
ccol_invalid_args        cache is CLRU_CACHE_NULL, or cache is a
                         stale handle that a call already destroyed, or
                         key_pair is NULL, or val_pair is NULL, or
                         val_pair->size is 0, or key_pair->size does
                         not agree with the width of the cache's key
                         type.
ccol_not_enough_memory   An allocation failed (the copy of the new
                         value, or the record of a new key). The
                         remote setter was not called, and nothing
                         changed for the key.
ccol_container_full      The cache's internal bookkeeping reached its
                         maximum element count while the function
                         stored a completely new key. A real program
                         cannot reach this.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
cache is an opaque VALUE handle, which the library resolves through a
slot table of its own before it touches the cache object behind it.
The resolve takes a pin, and the call holds it for its whole duration,
including any time spent blocked inside a remote setter callback, so
a concurrent clru_destroy() on the same handle waits for this call to
finish instead of freeing the cache under it.
The average-case complexity of the hash map operations is O(1).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clru_set (3),
.BR clrucache_get_full (3),
.BR clrucache_create_full (3)
